/-!
# Local complete intersections

This file proves two results from the section on local complete intersections.

The first says that a local complete intersection `S` over a field `k` is Cohen–Macaulay: every
local ring `S_𝔮` satisfies `depth(S_𝔮) = dim(S_𝔮)`. By Lemma 00SH each `S_𝔮` is a complete
intersection over `k`, that is `S_𝔮 ≅ R/(f_1, …, f_c)` with `R` a regular local ring and
`f_1, …, f_c` a regular sequence. A regular local ring is Cohen–Macaulay (Lemma 00NQ), and the
quotient of a Cohen–Macaulay local ring by a regular sequence is again Cohen–Macaulay
(Proposition 02JN).

The second is a descent statement for regular sequences along a commutative square of local rings
```
B ← S
↑   ↑
A ← R
```
in which `A = R/I` and `B = S/J`, the maps `R → S` and `A → B` are flat, and both `J ⊆ S` and its
image `J̄ ⊆ S̄ = S/𝔪_R S` are generated by regular sequences: then so is `I ⊆ R`. The argument
lifts a regular sequence `f̄_1, …, f̄_c̄` generating `J̄` to `f_1, …, f_c̄ ∈ J`. The quotient
`S/(f_1, …, f_c̄)` is flat over `R` (Lemma 00MG), so `S/((f_1, …, f_c̄) + IS)` is flat over `A`,
and comparing it with `B` modulo `𝔪_A` gives `J = (f_1, …, f_c̄) + IS` (Lemma 00ME). By Nakayama's
lemma `J` is then generated by the `f_i` together with elements `g_1, …, g_t ∈ I`, at most
`μ(J) - μ(J̄)` of them, where `μ` is the minimal number of generators. Faithful flatness of
`S/(f_1, …, f_c̄)` over `R` gives `I = (g_1, …, g_t)`. A dimension count finishes the proof: the
length of a regular sequence is the drop in dimension it produces, dimension is additive along flat
local homomorphisms (Lemma 00ON), and Krull's height theorem bounds `t` from below. It follows that
`dim(A) + t = dim(R)`, so `g_1, …, g_t` is a regular sequence because `R` is Cohen–Macaulay
(Proposition 02JN).

## Main results

* `IsCompleteIntersection.isMaximalCohenMacaulay`: a complete intersection over a field is a
  Cohen–Macaulay local ring.
* `IsLocalCompleteIntersection.isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localization`: a local complete
  intersection over a field is Cohen–Macaulay.
* `exists_isRegular_ofList_eq_ker_algebraMap_of_flat`: the descent of regular sequences along a
  flat square of local rings.
* `IsLocalRing.exists_subset_span_eq_ncard_le`: over a local ring, every generating set of a
  finitely generated submodule contains a generating subset with at most `spanFinrank` elements.

## Implementation notes

For the first result, Cohen–Macaulayness of the Noetherian ring `S` (Definition 00NC) is stated
at each localization `S_𝔮` at a prime, as `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay S_𝔮 S_𝔮`. The source
checks only the maximal ideals and appeals to the localization of Cohen–Macaulay rings; the
argument through Lemma 00SH treats every prime directly. The source proves the result by writing
`S_𝔪` as a quotient of the regular local ring `k[x_1, …, x_n]_{𝔪'}` by `c` elements and counting
dimensions. That dimension count is the content of Lemmas 00SC and 00SH, so here it enters
through Lemma 00SH. The finite type hypothesis on `S` is not imposed, since a local complete
intersection over `k` is of finite type over `k`.

For the second result the square is given by four algebras `R → S → B` and `R → A → B` with
`IsScalarTower R A B` and `IsScalarTower R S B`, and with `R → A` and `S → B` surjective, so that
`I` and `J` are the kernels of these two maps. The source's hypotheses are weakened in two ways,
and the statement is therefore more general than the source's.

* `R` is assumed to be a Cohen–Macaulay Noetherian local ring rather than a regular one. A regular
  local ring is Cohen–Macaulay (Lemma 00NQ), and Cohen–Macaulayness is all the argument uses.
* `S̄ = S/𝔪_R S` is not assumed to be regular. The source uses regularity of `S̄` only to compute
  the length `c̄` of a regular sequence generating `J̄` through Lemma 00SE. The equation
  `c̄ = dim(S̄) - dim(S̄/J̄)` holds for a regular sequence in any Noetherian local ring.

In the other direction, `S` is assumed to be Noetherian. The source works in the section's
Noetherian setting, and its appeals to Lemmas 00MG and 00ME both require this hypothesis. The
local rings `A` and `B` need no hypotheses of their own. Both are local, being nonzero quotients of
the local rings `R` and `S`, and all four maps of the square are local homomorphisms.

## References

* [Stacks 00SB](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00SB)
* [Stacks 02JP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02JP)
-/

